%N A292858 Numbers k such that 9 applications of 'Reverse and Subtract' lead to k, whereas fewer than 9 applications do not lead to k.
%C A292858 There are 9 forty-eight-digit terms in the sequence. Further terms are obtained (a) by inserting at the center of these terms either any number of 0's (for 111603518721165960373027269626940447783074704878, 176512193475025275151977319848516480415708873428, 637711546692957642526533655473763239712353991164, 647866614039059340696936459303056040158682342243, 766803951666578089253935450746129113344740601233) or any number of 9's (for the other four terms) and (b) by concatenating a term any number of times with itself and inserting an equal number of 0's at all junctures. Method (b) may be applied recursively to all terms. - Clarified by _Ray Chandler_, Oct 14 2017.
%C A292858 The terms are not all obtained from the 48-digit terms by the two constructions above. Number of 9-cycles by number of digits: 1 with 48 to 52, 2 with 54 to 60, 3 with 62 and 64, then 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13 with 66, 68, 70, 72, 74, 76, 78, 80. A new cycle, and thus new terms, appear with 54 digits (smallest term 111623209688158785189034946350568118412148114087674878). - _Frédéric Fichepain_, Oct 16 2026
%H A292858 Ray Chandler, <a href="/A292858/b292858.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..9</a>
%H A292858 J. H. E. Cohn, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/28-2/cohn.pdf">Palindromic differences</a>, Fibonacci Quart. 28 (1990), no. 2, 113-120.
%F A292858 n = f^9(n), n <> f^k(n) for k < 9, where f: x -> |x - reverse(x)|.
